(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-e-v1"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam (v1, archived) — Form E","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which statement about antimuscarinic agents is correct?","choices":["They act mainly at the skeletal neuromuscular junction","They block sympathetic adrenergic receptors as well as muscarinic ones","They do not block nicotinic receptors","They block nicotinic receptors at ganglia as their principal action"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they leave the actions of sympathetic stimulation unopposed. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which organisms is amphotericin B indicated against?","choices":["Cryptococcus, Blastomyces, Histoplasma, Candida, Coccidioides and Aspergillus, the last reserved for invasive infection","Dermatophyte infections of the skin, hair and nails only, with no activity against any of the systemic fungal organisms","Helminths including hookworm and pinworm","Influenza A and B"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Its breadth is why it remained the reference agent until voriconazole displaced it for aspergillosis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Why can high levels of a protein synthesis inhibitor produce host toxicity?","choices":["The drug is metabolized to a reactive intermediate that damages hepatocytes","Human cells lack the efflux pumps that protect bacteria from accumulation","Mammalian mitochondria use a 90S ribosome that binds these drugs preferentially at therapeutic levels","At high levels the drug may interact with mammalian 80S ribosomes rather than only the bacterial 70S ribosome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Selectivity is a matter of degree, not an absolute barrier, which is why these agents have dose-related toxicity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of the topical azole antifungals?","choices":["They bind fungal transfer RNA to stop protein synthesis","They inhibit fungal cytochrome P450, preventing cell wall formation","They inhibit ergosterol production directly","They inhibit uptake of molecular precursors at the fungal cell wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the named agents are clotrimazole, ketoconazole, miconazole and sertaconazole. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which receptors do labetalol and carvedilol block?","choices":["Beta-two only","Alpha-one, beta-one and beta-two","Beta-one only","Alpha-one and alpha-two only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the alpha-one component is what produces peripheral vasodilation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"What happens to acetylcholine after it binds its receptor?","choices":["It is metabolised by monoamine oxidase within the neuron","It diffuses away without enzymatic breakdown","It is degraded by acetylcholinesterase, and the choline is recycled","It is taken back into the presynaptic terminal intact and repackaged"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Degradation in the cleft, not reuptake of the intact transmitter, is the cholinergic pattern. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Two antifungal classes lower ergosterol and one binds it directly. Which is which?","choices":["Azoles and allylamines lower synthesis; polyenes bind ergosterol already in the membrane","Polyenes and azoles lower synthesis; allylamines bind ergosterol already present","Echinocandins and polyenes lower synthesis; azoles bind ergosterol","All three lower synthesis; none binds ergosterol directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The distinction matters because binding existing sterol acts immediately, while blocking synthesis depends on turnover. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which tumour do both phenoxybenzamine and phentolamine treat?","choices":["Carcinoid tumour","Small cell lung carcinoma","Insulinoma","Pheochromocytoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, a catecholamine-secreting tumour. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"What is the tetracycline mechanism of action?","choices":["Binding the 50S subunit and blocking transpeptidation","Inhibiting dihydrofolate reductase and blocking folate synthesis","Forming channels within the cell membrane that allow potassium and magnesium to leak out of the cell","Binding 16S ribosomal RNA of the 30S subunit, preventing transfer RNA from binding in the A site"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the result is bacteriostatic and broad-spectrum. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"How does acyclovir achieve selectivity for infected cells?","choices":["It is concentrated by a membrane transporter that is expressed only on the surface of infected cells","It binds a viral capsid protein that has no human counterpart","It is activated only at the low pH found inside infected cells","It is selectively phosphorylated by viral thymidine kinase, so it is only activated where the virus is replicating"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Selectivity comes from an enzyme the virus supplies, which is the cleanest form of selective toxicity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Which effects predominate with ganglionic blockade?","choices":["Vasoconstriction with bradycardia and increased secretions","Bronchodilation with hyperglycaemia and tremor","Miosis with salivation and diarrhoea","Vasodilation, with atony of the bladder and gut and tachycardia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, since each organ loses whichever division normally dominates it. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which linezolid interactions can produce serotonin syndrome?","choices":["Iron, calcium and dairy products","Ethanol, which produces a disulfiram-like reaction with flushing and vomiting","Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, tyramine-containing foods, and pseudoephedrine","Warfarin, phenytoin and digoxin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and platelets are monitored separately because of thrombocytopenia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which sites does herpes simplex type 1 characteristically affect?","choices":["Retina, oesophagus and colon","Skin in a dermatomal distribution following chickenpox","Mouth, face, skin, oesophagus or brain","Genitals, rectum, hands or meninges"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Type 2 covers genitals, rectum, hands or meninges — the split is anatomical rather than absolute. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"What is neostigmine's absorption and central penetration?","choices":["It is well absorbed orally and readily enters the central nervous system","It is given only intravenously because it is destroyed by gastric acid","It penetrates the central nervous system and is used for Alzheimer disease","Poorly absorbed orally, and it does not enter the central nervous system"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is what keeps its effects peripheral. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Why did competitive neuromuscular blockers make anaesthesia safer?","choices":["They shorten the duration of surgery","They eliminate the need for intubation","They prevent malignant hyperthermia","They allow the use of less anaesthetic agent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The relaxation is achieved without deepening the anaesthetic. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is happening to oseltamivir resistance?","choices":["Resistance occurs only in influenza B","It has been becoming a problem over time","Resistance has never been reported","Resistance is prevented entirely by starting within 48 hours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the same trajectory the antibacterial section describes for the fluoroquinolones. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Why is topical acne therapy applied to the whole area rather than to spots?","choices":["Spot application increases the risk of systemic absorption","The vehicle is only stable when spread thinly over a wide area","Individual lesions are already resolving by the time they are visible","Topical therapy only works where it is applied"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and that is why the whole affected area is treated rather than visible lesions alone. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which fluoroquinolone must not be used for urinary tract infection?","choices":["All three are equally suitable for urinary tract infection","Moxifloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","Levofloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It also lacks Pseudomonas coverage, unlike levofloxacin, and needs no renal dose adjustment. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which topical antibiotic is preferred for acne, and what is happening to the alternative?","choices":["Clindamycin is preferred; erythromycin is losing efficacy to Propionibacterium acnes resistance","Erythromycin is preferred; clindamycin is losing efficacy to resistance","Mupirocin is the preferred agent for acne; bacitracin is losing efficacy to Propionibacterium acnes resistance","Neomycin is preferred; polymyxin B is losing efficacy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and both are noted to lack systemic side effects. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A transplant recipient develops cytomegalovirus retinitis. Why is acyclovir not the answer?","choices":["Acyclovir's indications are herpes simplex and varicella-zoster; cytomegalovirus requires ganciclovir or valganciclovir","Acyclovir is available only intravenously and cannot safely be given to a transplant recipient on immunosuppression","Acyclovir causes neutropenia in a third of patients","Acyclovir has no antiviral activity at all in immunocompromised patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All are guanine analogues, but the indications are drawn along virus lines rather than by drug family. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Augmentin and Unasyn are the drugs of choice for which infections?","choices":["Syphilis and gas gangrene","Skin and soft tissue infection, diabetic foot infection, and animal or human bites","Neutropenic fever and hospital-acquired pneumonia","Clostridium difficile colitis, intra-abdominal abscess, and pseudomembranous colitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The added Bacteroides and methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus coverage is what fits these polymicrobial sites. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How do the indirect-acting adrenergic agonists work?","choices":["They increase synthesis of norepinephrine from tyrosine","They release norepinephrine, or block its reuptake","They bind postsynaptic receptors and mimic norepinephrine","They inhibit catechol-O-methyltransferase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and they do not act on postsynaptic receptors directly. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A patient is starting a topical azole. How long is the course?","choices":["Treatment continues until the rash disappears, then stops immediately","Treatment must continue for at least six months","Treatment is generally prolonged, on the order of two to three weeks","A single application is usually curative"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and stopping early is how a treated infection returns. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which infections are acyclovir and valacyclovir indicated for?","choices":["Human immunodeficiency virus infection","Herpes simplex and varicella-zoster","Cytomegalovirus retinitis in transplant recipients","Influenza A and B"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cytomegalovirus requires ganciclovir or valganciclovir instead. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"How many steps are there in adrenergic neurotransmission?","choices":["Three","Ten","Five","Six"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, against the six sequential steps given for the cholinergic neuron. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which two groups must not receive tetracyclines?","choices":["Children under eight years and pregnant women","Adolescents and the elderly","Patients with eczema and patients with psoriasis","Patients taking oral contraceptives and patients on spironolactone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These are the two. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is phentolamine's non-cardiovascular use?","choices":["Treatment of impotence","Treatment of glaucoma","Treatment of asthma","Treatment of narcolepsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, injected to dilate penile arteries. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which body sites are more permeable to a topical drug?","choices":["Back, chest, abdomen and buttocks","Shins, forearms, dorsal hands and neck","Scrotum, face, axilla and scalp","Palms, soles, knees and elbows"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Regional variation in penetration is the first variable, and these four are its examples. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why does stopping propranolol abruptly risk arrhythmias?","choices":["Beta receptors have been up-regulated","Beta receptors have been permanently destroyed","Norepinephrine stores have been depleted","The drug accumulates and causes late toxicity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so the heart is left hypersensitive to catecholamines. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"Which receptor does dobutamine selectively stimulate?","choices":["Beta-one","Beta-two","Alpha-one","Alpha-two"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, a synthetic selective agonist. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Catecholamines barely enter the brain, yet cause central-type effects. Which?","choices":["Depression, nightmares and memory loss","Hallucinations, delirium and seizures","Anxiety, tremor and headache","Sedation, lethargy and constipation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They are described as CNS-like effects despite the drugs being too polar to enter the brain. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What are the antipseudomonal penicillins' adverse reactions?","choices":["Disulfiram-like reaction with ethanol and metallic taste","Hypersensitivity reaction, rash, and diarrhoea","Ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity, and red man syndrome","Tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy, and central nervous system toxicity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with monitoring for anaphylaxis, renal function, complete blood count and bleeding. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which adverse effects and interactions accompany griseofulvin?","choices":["Inhibition of cytochrome P450 3A4, producing raised plasma levels of co-administered substrate drugs and a risk of toxicity","Nephrotoxicity and electrolyte wasting","Visual disturbance in 30 percent with hepatic and renal effects","Headache in about 15 percent, mental confusion, fatigue and blurred vision, with induction of cytochrome P450 1A2 and 2C9"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Note it induces enzymes where the azoles inhibit them — opposite directions of interaction within the same broad category of drug. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"How often is imiquimod applied?","choices":["Two to five times per week","Twice daily without interruption","Once monthly","Only at night, because it is photolabile"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is a weekly schedule rather than a daily one. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which three agents make up the echinocandin class?","choices":["Amphotericin B, nystatin and natamycin","Naftifine, terbinafine and tolnaftate","Fluconazole, voriconazole and posaconazole","Caspofungin, micafungin and anidulafungin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three inhibit 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase in the cell wall. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"What kind of alpha blockade does phenoxybenzamine produce?","choices":["Selective for alpha-two only","Irreversible and noncompetitive","Competitive and reversible","Selective for alpha-one only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so recovery needs new receptor synthesis, taking at least a day. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"Which two enzymes inactivate the catecholamines, and where does each act?","choices":["Catechol-O-methyltransferase postsynaptically and monoamine oxidase within the neuron","Acetylcholinesterase in the cleft and pseudocholinesterase in plasma","Monoamine oxidase postsynaptically and catechol-O-methyltransferase in plasma","Cytochrome P450 in the liver alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and rapid inactivation by both is why catecholamines are short acting. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"How does concentration-dependent killing differ from time-dependent killing?","choices":["In concentration-dependent killing, activity depends on renal clearance; in time-dependent killing it depends on hepatic metabolism","In concentration-dependent killing, higher drug concentration produces greater killing and there is a post-antibiotic effect; in time-dependent killing the extent of killing plateaus and the time above the minimal inhibitory concentration governs the result","In concentration-dependent killing the drug must be given by continuous infusion to hold a steady level; in time-dependent killing a single large daily dose is preferred because the post-antibiotic effect carries the interval","In concentration-dependent killing, the drug is bacteriostatic; in time-dependent killing the drug is bactericidal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Time above the minimal inhibitory concentration should be 40 to 70 percent of the dosing interval for time-dependent agents. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which forms of shock is dopamine used for?","choices":["Cardiogenic and septic","Anaphylactic and haemorrhagic","Neurogenic only","Hypovolaemic only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"What are the indications for the echinocandins?","choices":["Dermatophyte infection of the nails and scalp","Zygomycete infection such as Mucor","Oesophageal candidiasis, systemic aspergillosis not responding to itraconazole or amphotericin B, and febrile neutropenic patients not responding to antibiotics","Cryptococcal meningitis, oral and vaginal candidiasis, and prophylaxis in advanced human immunodeficiency virus infection, together with urinary candidiasis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — largely a salvage and neutropenic-fever role rather than first-line therapy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which organism has increasingly rendered the penicillinase-resistant penicillins ineffective, and what replaces them?","choices":["Extended-spectrum beta-lactamase producing organisms, for which carbapenems are preferred","Pseudomonas aeruginosa, for which cefepime is preferred","Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, for which vancomycin is the treatment of choice","Vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus, for which linezolid is the treatment of choice"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The class was built for Staphylococcus aureus and methicillin resistance is precisely what defeats it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does pilocarpine do in the eye?","choices":["Miosis and ciliary muscle contraction, lowering intraocular pressure","Causes mydriasis and cycloplegia, raising intraocular pressure","Reduces production of aqueous humour without changing pupil size","Has no ocular effect and is used only for dry mouth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with onset within minutes and a duration of several hours. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which adverse effects are listed for posaconazole?","choices":["Headache in about 15 percent of patients, with mental confusion and blurred vision","Bone marrow suppression and hepatotoxicity","QT prolongation, fever, diarrhoea, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and thrombocytopenia","Visual disturbance in about 30 percent of patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The QT effect places it alongside the macrolides and fluoroquinolones as a drug to watch in combination. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"How are the two sections of an autonomic efferent pathway described?","choices":["A postganglionic neuron in the cord and a preganglionic neuron in the periphery","A preganglionic neuron in the central nervous system and a postganglionic neuron from a ganglion","A single myelinated neuron running from the central nervous system directly to the effector organ","Two preganglionic neurons in series, both within the central nervous system"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the postganglionic neuron is generally nonmyelinated. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"For a time-dependent agent with no post-antibiotic effect, what dosing strategy applies?","choices":["A single large daily dose, taking advantage of the prolonged post-antibiotic effect","A loading dose followed by widely spaced maintenance doses, with the interval guided by trough levels alone rather than by time above the minimal inhibitory concentration","Alternate-day dosing to allow tissue concentrations to accumulate between doses","Continuous or frequent infusions, keeping the concentration above the minimal inhibitory concentration for 40 to 70 percent of the dosing interval"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Without a post-antibiotic effect, organisms resume growing as soon as the level falls, so time above the threshold is what matters. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which adverse effects do the older bladder agents cause?","choices":["Dry mouth, constipation and blurred vision","Bradycardia, bronchospasm and diarrhoea","Fasciculations and muscle weakness","Rebound hypertension and sedation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which are the predictable antimuscarinic effects. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A patient on imiquimod reports redness, swelling and erosions at the site. How should this be interpreted?","choices":["It means the applied dose is too low and the frequency should be increased until the skin clears","Skin irritation occurs in virtually all patients, and the degree of inflammation parallels efficacy","It indicates an allergic reaction and the drug should be stopped","It is unusual and suggests secondary bacterial infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so the reaction is expected and is not by itself a reason to stop. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What is the rank order of potency at alpha receptors?","choices":["Isoproterenol greater than epinephrine greater than norepinephrine","Norepinephrine greater than isoproterenol greater than epinephrine","All three are equipotent at alpha receptors","Epinephrine at least equal to norepinephrine, far above isoproterenol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the beta order runs the other way with isoproterenol first. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"How long does terbinafine treatment take for fingernail and toenail infection?","choices":["One week for fingernails and one month for toenails","Six to nine months for fingernails and up to twelve months for toenails","A single dose for both sites","Six to twelve weeks for fingernails and up to twelve months for toenails"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. As with griseofulvin, the duration follows nail growth, which is the counselling point that keeps patients on therapy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"What warning attaches to polymyxin B?","choices":["Avoid it in pregnancy and in breastfeeding, because systemic absorption through inflamed skin is teratogenic","Avoid it in children under eight because it stains teeth","Avoid it with benzoyl peroxide because it is inactivated","Avoid using it on open wounds or denuded skin in high doses, because of neurotoxicity and nephrotoxicity risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loss of the barrier is what turns a topical agent into a systemic exposure. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Where are M2 receptors found?","choices":["Adrenal medulla and the neuromuscular junction","Neurons, cardiac cells and smooth muscle","Neurons and gastric parietal cells","Neurons, bladder, exocrine glands and smooth muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why muscarinic stimulation slows the heart. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How does pyrantel pamoate kill its target parasites?","choices":["It inhibits formation of helminth microtubules and blocks glucose uptake","It inhibits squalene epoxidase in the parasite, progressively depleting the membrane of ergosterol until it loses integrity","It blocks neuraminidase and prevents the parasite from leaving host cells","It releases acetylcholine and inhibits cholinesterase, acting as a depolarizing neuromuscular blocker that causes paralysis and death"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is used for pinworm and hookworm. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"What is the echinocandin mechanism of action?","choices":["Formation of membrane channels allowing potassium to leak from the cell","Disruption of the mitotic spindle, halting cell division","Inhibition of 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase, lowering formation of 1,3-beta-D-glucan in the cell wall","Inhibition of lanosterol demethylase, progressively reducing ergosterol in the fungal membrane"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Caspofungin, micafungin and anidulafungin are the three agents, and the wall target is unique among the antifungals here. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which organisms cause systemic mycoses affecting internal organs?","choices":["Mucor, Rhizopus and Pneumocystis only","Aspergillus, Blastomyces, Candida, Coccidioides, Cryptococcus, Histoplasma and Zygomycetes","Epidermophyton, Microsporum and Trichophyton","Chromomycosis, Pseudallescheriasis and Sporotrichosis affecting the deeper skin layers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the organisms the systemic antifungals target, which is why amphotericin B's indication list closely mirrors this one. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which antiviral does NOT act on viral nucleic acid?","choices":["Ganciclovir, which inhibits in a manner similar to acyclovir","Penciclovir, which has a mechanism similar to acyclovir","Oseltamivir, which inhibits neuraminidase to block viral release","Acyclovir, which is incorporated into viral DNA"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The guanine analogues all terminate the nucleic acid chain; oseltamivir works at a completely different stage. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which serious dermatologic adverse effects are listed for the aminopenicillins?","choices":["Red man syndrome during infusion","Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis","Photosensitivity and permanent discoloration of the teeth","Tendonitis and rupture of the Achilles tendon"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These sit alongside hepatic dysfunction, Clostridium difficile infection, interstitial nephritis and haematologic effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin is preferred in neonatal fever or sepsis, and which is restricted in the first 30 days of life?","choices":["Cefotaxime is preferred; ceftriaxone cannot be used in the first 30 days of life","Ceftriaxone is preferred; cefotaxime cannot be used in the first 30 days of life","Ceftazidime is preferred; cefepime cannot be used in the first 30 days of life","Cefdinir is preferred; cefixime cannot be used in the first 30 days of life"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pairing is worth holding together, because the two agents are otherwise close substitutes. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are the three general types of signalling between cells?","choices":["Neurotransmitters, receptors and second messengers","Afferent, efferent and enteric signalling","Hormones, local mediators and neurotransmitters","Hormones, enzymes and ion channels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the neurotransmitters of interest here are acetylcholine, norepinephrine and epinephrine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which haematologic adverse effects limit ganciclovir?","choices":["Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia","Megaloblastic anaemia and agranulocytosis from folate antagonism","Haemolysis triggered by glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","Isolated eosinophilia with no clinical consequence"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and about a third of patients must stop intravenous treatment because of side effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which pigmentary adverse effect does azelaic acid cause?","choices":["Slate-grey discolouration of the nail beds","Yellow staining of the sclera","Hypopigmentation","Hyperpigmentation of sun-exposed areas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is listed with mild skin irritation and dryness. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 22"}]};
